course: dictionary
lesson: 3336
title: Clues that show what is true
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Evidently is a small word that tells you how sure the writer is. It usually means clearly or obviously, because you can see the signs. «It was the Rat, and he was evidently laughing—the Mole could feel him laughing, right down his arm and through his paw.» The Mole could feel the laughing, so it was clear that the Rat was laughing.
explain: Evidently can also mean apparently, which means it seems that something is true, because of what you have heard or worked out. «But you are evidently in sore trouble and distress, so I will not desert you.» The speaker can tell from the signs that the other person is in trouble, so the trouble is clear from what they can see and hear.
explain: To check the meaning, swap in clearly or it seems that. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Detectives and story writers use evidently to show a conclusion drawn from clues.
fact: Evidently means clearly or obviously, because the signs show it.
fact: Evidently can also mean apparently, which means it seems that, when you have heard something or worked it out.
fact: Useful phrases: was evidently, evidently not.
fact: «Evidently, someone had been in the kitchen.» The writer knows this from clues, not from seeing the person.
fact: Good for detective stories and for reading, where you work out what has happened from clues.
exercise: «It was the Rat, and he was evidently laughing.» What does evidently mean here? | *Clearly, you could tell from the signs | Slowly, one step at a time | Quietly, without making a sound | Sadly, with tears
exercise: Ella sees muddy paw prints on the stairs and an empty dog bed. She says, «Evidently, the dog has been out.» What does evidently suggest? | Muddy stairs are a kind of game | The dog is sad about something | *It seems the dog has been out, from the clues | The dog is asleep upstairs
exercise: Find the word that means clearly in «But you are evidently in sore trouble and distress, so I will not desert you.» | *evidently | distress | desert | trouble
test: «The gate was evidently open.» Which word could replace evidently here? | *clearly | sadly | never | quietly
test: «Evidently, someone had been in the kitchen.» What does this sentence suggest? | Nobody has ever been in the kitchen | The kitchen is clean and tidy | A cooking show is on TV | *The clues show that someone was there
exam: «The door was evidently locked, so we went round the back.» What does evidently mean here, and how do you know? | Painted blue, so you could see it | Open wide, with nobody inside | Loudly rattled by the wind | *Clearly locked, because we had to go round the back
